uh  foreign  okay  thank you for joining us the conclusions  have been adopted and we will publish  them shortly like with a yesterday  president of the european council please  merci merci beaucoup  we indeed  made every  i want to encourage every effort take  every  measure to bring  this war to an end  uh as regards the key points we covered  today the food security was a  particularly important point we invited  president maki sal  who is the  president of the african union currently  to discuss with african representatives  how the european union and the african  union could cooperate as effectively  as possible  to meet this  challenge that we jointly face to take  measures  to support  the african countries in the spirit of  partnership to help improve strengthen  the production capacities in africa  and also to ensure  supporting measures  so that one can help avert  this  potentially serious crisis for a number  of countries it could have a  a a harmful effect in africa but also in  europe that's one element and you see  the detailed conclusions of that  uh today is the energy and we all know  this is a fundamental challenge for all  of us just extremely important on the on  the one hand to discuss uh on the  proposals  put on the table by the commission in  order to to prepare the following steps  we power eu is a very important  initiative we had the occasion to  exchange views uh and to uh and to make  progress on that uh topic and especially  we understand this is extremely  important to um work uh on  on the possibility and and to explore  the possibility of import price caps  also with international partners we  intend also to act in four key areas the  diversification diversified fuels and  sources  in the renewables to speed up renewables  is of course  fundamental but also to work in the  field of energy efficiency to save  energy  is of course a fundamental priority for  all of us and we are also determined to  invest in infrastructure in energy  interconnections and of course in  renewable we need also to improve our  preparedness for possible major supply  disruptions and we have also  asked the commission to work on the  optimization of the functioning of the  european electricity  market  so the  on energy was an opportunity to reaffirm  our determination  to pick up this challenge as europeans  now we face this challenge before the  war in ukraine but even more so now  because of the ongoing war in ukraine  and then finally  the third point  i wanted to to mention concerning  european defense you will see the  conclusions too  in this field  we share the view share the view around  the table that we need to spend and  invest more on defense and to do so not  just more but also better  to act more effectively better together  as europeans  for involving for example grouped uh  procurement and also  our serious effort to strengthen  europe's industrial  base in this field taking account in  particular of the role of european smes  which can play  a key role in this process it was also  an opportunity to reaffirm  the importance of  what  complementarity between what the eu does  and what nato does there will be a  summit in madrid  and this would  no doubt be an opportunity to reaffirm  this complementarity between the  european union and nato so these are  some of the key points i wanted to share  with you  in the immediate aftermath of the  european council which has just  concluded madam precedent  you might remember that in versailles  the leaders decided to phase out the  dependency on russian fossil fuels as  soon as possible  we started with coal  yesterday in the middle of the night we  decided then to have a ban now on de  facto 90 percent of russian oil  imports to the european union by the end  of the year  and this comes at a time when we see  that russia has disrupted supplies to by  now five member states  um you know finland bulgaria and  poland  but now to a company in the netherlands  and to a company in denmark  so our answer has to be very clear how  are we going to manage  and what is the road map to really  get rid of the dependency of russian  fossil fuels and here with view to gas  the answer is repower eu  it brings basically three different  pillars um that we have discussed today  the first one is the diversification  away from russian fossil fuels  specifically on gas  here we now have set up a joint task  force for common purchases joint  purchases of gas because the market  power of the whole of 27 in the european  union is much bigger than every single  member state and we will achieve better  conditions  we have of course already since the  beginning of the year started to look  out for other more reliable suppliers  and this effort is already paying off we  see it today that the lng deliveries  from other parts of the world others  than russia  have doubled in queue one in 2022  compared for example to the previous  year  the second element we are working on in  repower eu is the security of supply  through better interconnections  so that the gas can flow wherever it is  needed  and of course the common objective to  have a more strategic  storage across a gas storage across the  european union here we have good news  our gas storage is already filled at 41  of the capacity this is five percentage  points higher than it was the case last  year  at the same date  and then the third is the most important  pillar and this is the massive  investment in renewables it's  accelerating the deployment of  renewables across the european union  the renewable energy has the big  advantage that it is not only good for  the climate  but it is also good for our independence  and good for our security of supply and  it creates jobs at home  so for this plan  we power eu  we have been discussing with the council  european council we are proposing to  support it with 300 billion euros from  eu funding there are different  parts that are contributing to this  funding if there are any questions about  me i'm very happy to respond to them but  this is the house of repower eu that we  have presented and discussed today  basically it is solidarity and  cooperation that are at the heart of any  successful strategy to deal  with russia as a non-reliable supplier  anymore  cooperation for example as has been  demonstrated by denmark germany the  netherlands and belgium  who  a couple of days ago  showed at a joint wind summit what it  means to develop develop the wind power  the offshore wind power in the north sea  and of course solidarity this was a big  topic today ensuring that in case of a  full disruption of gas flows from russia  gas is indeed allowed to flow to  wherever it is needed in europe  the second topic  was this defense  the war again  wasn't is a stark reminder  for our member states on the need to  strengthen our defence capacities  we have seen positive developments  notably of course we are all aware of  finland's and sweden's application to  join nato nato the strongest military  alliance in the world  and they will benefit greatly from their  membership  but also the fact that since the  beginning of the war  leaders and the member states stepped up  and announced by now 200 billion euros  in extra military spending  now it is important that we ensure that  these  stepping up and additional investment  we get the maximum value out of it  and we all know the challenges that  exist  within the european union or the  european model  on one hand it is the lack of of  investment over the last years so the  gaps have to be filled  but the bigger challenge is the  fragmentation we have within the  european defense industry and the  dublication of weapon systems  here we see that this leads to increased  cost to reduce interoperability so we  have to improve this situation  and therefore it is first of all helpful  that since the beginning of this mandate  the european defense fund is up and  running it's a strong incentive to work  together  the pilot program so far  have benefited to more than 600 entities  and 26 member states have used so far  the european defense fund this is an  encouraging start  but there's also a second element now  when we replenish  the military material that has been sent  to ukraine for example it is important  that this is coordinated and for that  we're setting up a task force  to coordinate also to deconflict the  replenishment of the national stockpiles  and the commission will propose before  end of june to mobilize 500 million  euros over the next two years from the  eu budget to incentivize  the joint procurement by at least three  member states  this is let's say a pilot for a longer  term proposal that will come  in the course of this year for a  european defense investment program  finally  the third topic  was on food security charles introduced  in the whole topic already at the moment  being we're facing one overriding  urgency and that is 20 million tons of  wheat are stuck in ukraine and they have  to get out  and therefore we have created and we're  working hard on the solidarity lines  lanes  that will enable it to bring out parts  of this wheat through land route and  trains towards our parts  it is not trivial and it is of course  more tedious and expensive but it is  necessary to get this wheat out  the second point that is important that  we now give relief to the vulnerable  populations and um  the possibility to afford the food  for that we have pledged two and a half  billion euros  and we mobilize uh we propose to  mobilize reserves from the european  development fund to support for example  sub-saharan africa  the third element in that is that we  step up our own food production  we expect a record export of cereals of  40 million tons in 22 and 23.  within this scenario  we call on all partners not to restrict  global trade on agricultural products  so any kind of trade restrictions have  absolutely no place in our member states  or globally  and finally  but this is the mid and long term we  really have to develop  better strategies uh for example in  north africa and sub-saharan africa to  increase the production and the  resilience with new technologies  precision farming the nanotechnologies  that are there so a lot of topics that  are combined to this food topic  from the urgency to the mid and to the  long term  we will coordinate all our actions with  the team europe approach  to feed into this work  i will travel to cairo mid-june  to discuss all these issues with  president al-sisi  because we have to look at them not only  from a european ukraine perspective but  also from a regional and bilateral  perspective many thanks  thank you madam president  okay thomas you are next  desolate secretary  export from from ukraine do you see any  chances that all these solidarity lines  and calls for the eu member states to  for help in in the export could add up  to the capacity sufficient for exporting  ukrainian with this year this this this  summer  if not because it seems for now it's too  too too little what would be your  comment on these ideas of of  transporting the wheat through  through belarus  uh on the price of it through various  baltic states and if not do you see any  diplomatic possibility to  convince or to make uh russians to to  unblock odessa or to export the wheat  thank you no you are absolutely right it  should be the the first priority because  there are 22 millions of tons of grains  that are blocked in ukraine especially  in odessa and a few weeks ago when i  went to this site the occasion to see  with my own ice was the situation on the  ground and for instance countries like  egypt and other countries  in africa  they are victims of this this situation  it means that we fully support all the  efforts made by the united nations in  order to find an agreement  uh to open a maritime corridor to the  black through the black sea it would be  the best option in order to make sure it  is possible to export grains but we are  not certain we are certain it will work  we hope it'll be possible to get an  agreement on that important topic and  that's why we are also working based on  the proposal of the commission on  those  green lanes  in order to facilitate alternative  routes and with the leaders  today and yesterday we discussed the  different possibilities in order to  develop alternative routes but we know  that it's more difficult it's more  expensive it's more difficult for  obvious logistic  reasons  and we try to facilitate  in order to in order to uh to reduce the  initiative burdens which also which is  also an important question about belarus  and the access  towards the baltic sea  we understand that there are also tolls  at the level of the united nations on  that on that topic  and we support all these efforts but  in the short term we don't see a  concrete a concrete result a concrete  achievement and you know that we have  decided uh sanctions on belarus on the  on the potassium fertilizers uh for  instance and we finns is very important  to maintain the pressure against the  kremlin but also against belarus because  we feel that this country is helping a  lot uh russia  in the in in the framework of this war  launched by russia against ukraine yeah  um only little to add um  charles described indeed our  efforts or the international efforts in  the black sea  it is important not to underestimate the  work we can do now and we are doing now  indeed these 20 million tons are there  on average ukraine exported in normal  times 5 million tons per month  now we are at least at a range between  200 000 and 1 million ton per month with  these measures of the green lanes for  example very tedious very difficult  lots of effort but it's something you  can do right now and we are doing right  now while the other efforts for the  bigger picture of course are still  ongoing  yuri  you are next  uh yes thank you very much yuri shikoh  ukrainian service of deutsche welle uh i  have a question first from about the  embargo on oil  it of course it's great that the you  reach this deal but  if you look at the message it sends to  russia it looks like it says to russia  that russia has still time six eight  more months to continue destroying  ukraine till the embargo starts  enters into force  and it says like russia has all this  time in order to find other buyers for  this oil so my question is uh which  message do you see it sends to  russia and how it helps to stop war now  and the second question is also because  you discussed today a lot of  energy uh what do you see ukraine is  connected to  uh european continental electricity grid  already for a couple of months  uh and i know that the work is going on  the commercial of selling of ukrainian  electricity to the eu countries so what  which perspective do you see i think  it's rather too  far from the lion question which  perspective do you see here how  soon it might start this commercial  trade of electricity with ukraine and  how can it help also europe in its  energy transition thank you very much i  think it was an outstanding success that  we were able to connect the electricity  grid that fast  you must know that  in february and february beginning of  march there was a planned  decoupling  of the ukrainian electricity grid from  the russian electricity grid with  initially the plan to recouple again and  take two years to then join the european  union  of course when the war hit it was  absolutely impossible to recouple  ukraine to the russian grid  and we managed within two weeks  to couple the ukrainian grid to the  european european one and this was and  is a big success  and therefore  i think it also shows that we have  ongoing war work during the war  that always um improves and supports the  situation of ukraine so it's very  important not only to stare at the war  which is atrocious and horrible  but to never give up on doing the daily  work to improve the situation of ukraine  itself for your wider question  um i think we have to look at two points  um it is a wind down period  uh in six to eight months till the end  of the year so it's not an abrupt and  full speed  uh supply during that time and then  abrupt end but  a clear reduction of oil already  one  figure i have on the top of my head is  for example germany  told us that since the beginning of the  year where they started with 35 russian  oil they are already down to 12  russian oil this shows that by just  working hard on not renewing contracts  finishing contracts and looking at other  suppliers you can fill this wind down  period  much better and most importantly in the  back is of course the whole big package  of six sanctions and these sanctions are  biting hard  and uh are really um  destroying and that's the the goal uh  the basis the financial basis of putin  leading this war and hitting hard the  russian economy  i would like to also to a few words  about this important question because uh  i think that when we were in versailles  a few weeks ago not many observers would  have thought that it would have been  possible for us only in a few weeks  to take such a concrete and strange  decision  we have decided to sanctions russia by  banning  old is extremely important and we will  succeed in the following months to  implement that decision and ursula  is right when she said that it will  break the financing of the russian war  machine this is our goal and we have two  tools that we have that we are using as  much as we can on the one hand we  support ukraine with military treatment  with financial support with material  with political support as much as we can  also when we engage with third countries  in order to have  more support at the international level  point one and point two we have decided  on five packages of sanctions and the  last one was the the that we have  decided yesterday what's more difficult  because this is about energy and energy  is a big topic it's a big thing for the  members for the households for the  families for the businesses and that's  why we have decided to work on solutions  on concrete solutions in order to make  sure that the decisions we are taking  are painful for russia for the kremlin  this is our main goal  daniel question philip  passes  sorry a bit of a trip to get here from  le soir question for both of you  do you share the view of certain member  states that with this sixth package  it's time to take a break when it comes  to the sanctions  to look at what the effects of these  sanctions are because it's also got a  price tag or do you think as others  believe that it's now time to prepare  the way for the seventh package now you  said yesterday that corporate on  wednesday would probably complete all  the work so the sixth package could  enter into force already on wednesday or  thursday can you confirm that date the  second question is with regard to food  security  moscow keeps repeating that uh the cause  of all this evil is of course the  western sanctions the fact that we're  not letting their vessels  into our ports and  also  what's happening around  odessa do you think that that is a  massive trap  they want to de-mine odessa so that they  can then get access themselves to the  port well on that less question on  odessa  of course there has to be a security  guarantee there we can't have a maritime  corridor for humanitarian reasons  weakening the security situation in  ukraine obviously we would support the  position of ukraine each and every time  they try to defend their legitimate  security concerns  and secondly yesterday we had a debate  which had been prepared and the run-up  to that for the sixth package of  sanctions and we are now focusing on the  implementation of this six package now  that doesn't mean that there are  opinions out there  different members of the council have  different opinions but we are focusing  now on this sixth package and as i  indicated yesterday uh the legal texts  are being drafted and the procedure  therefore can take off as of tomorrow so  the text will be sent to or invalidated  tomorrow evening or the day after so it  could just be a question of hours  with the legal drafting of the text but  i can confirm that as of  tomorrow where the legal texts will be  there and can be addressed to all the  corrupter members  the question is concerned on  six package and the follow-up  i think  on one hand we have to look at the  russian economy and financial market  and on the other hand on  the big resources to the russian  government that is energy  fossil fuel energy  and we have now clearly addressed  the three topics of coal oil and gas  with two sanctioned packages  and repower eu  and the six sanctioned packages are also  of course addressing the financial  sector and  hurting it heavily the same goes for the  russian economy  and for me is important now after having  put in place the structure on the energy  element that we look in the financial  and the economic sector of russia  whether there are attempts of  circumventions now  or trying to move away from this  situation  and therefore i would not exclude that  we over and over will have to redress  the situation we address the situation  that we close loopholes that we stop  circumventions so that we keep on  working on that  but the big bulk on energy this is up  and running this is on the road in what  we've just described  on the  food looming food crisis  that the fact that there is a severe  food crisis developing  is  only the fault of russia's unjustified  war  without this war we would not have it  and we should always over and over  repeat that um  russia is not only blocking the expert  of export of  ukrainian wheat it is bombarding  warehouses where wheat is stored  deliberately  of course you know that they are also  mining the fields where ukraine could  have the next harvest  of corn  and i want to be very clear we have no  sanctions on food and agricultural  products no sanctions on that  and important also if you is speaking  about fertilizers for example third  countries have access to all the  fertilizers for example from russia  this was a clear decision  that we say humanitarian goods and food  agriculture wheat of course is a part of  it  no sanctions on it so it's a complete  misinformation from russia's side the  only reason  why we are struggling now with the food  crisis is because of this brutal  unjustified war against ukraine  thank you madam president this concludes  our press conference it's a matter of  minutes for the publication of the  conclusions  excuse me

